    <article>
        <articleInfo lang="EN" FirstPage="115" LastPage="125">
            <pdf>26305-jdecp-mukhin.pdf</pdf>
            <abstract>An algorithm for the joint finding matrices of the Lyapunov function and feedback in quadratic stabilization solving problems of linear time-invariant systems with a partially measurable state vector is proposed. The basis for the algorithm is the bilinear matrix inequalities linearization and two mutually inverse matrixes finding. It is proved that the numerical sequence generated by the algorithm is converged. The algorithm testing is performed on completely controlled and completely observed systems, generated randomly. It is additionally shown the algorithm applying for the two-link inverted pendulum stabilization problem solving by the dynamic feedback.</abstract>
            <title>An algorithm for the joint finding matrices of the Lyapunov function and feedback in linear systems quadratic stabilization problems</title>
            <keywords>
                <word>Lyapunov function matrix</word>
                <word>quadratic stabilization</word>
                <word>feedback</word>
                <word>bilinear matrix inequalities</word>
            </keywords>
            <references>
                <reference>Balandin D.V., Kogan M.M. Synthesis of control law regulators based on linear matrix inequalities. M.: FIZMATLIT, 2007.</reference>
                <reference>Boyd S., El Ghaoui L., Feron E., Balakrishnan V. Linear Matrix Inequalities in System and Control Theory. Philadelphia: SIAM, 1994.</reference>
                <reference>Nesterov Y. E., Nemirovski A. Interior-Point Polynomial Algorithms in Convex Programming. Philadelphia: SIAM, 1994.</reference>
                <reference>Toker O., Ozbay H. On the NP hardness of solving bilinear matrix inequalities and simultaneous stabilization with static output feedback. Proc. of the American Control Conference, vol. 4, p. 2525-2526, 1995.</reference>
                <reference>Hassibi A., How J., Boyd S. A path-following method for solving BMI problems in control. Proc. of American Control Conference, vol. 2, p. 1385-1389, 1999.</reference>
                <reference>Dinh T.Q., Gumussoy S., Michiels W., Diehl M. Combining convex–concave decompositions and linearization approaches for solving BMIs, with application to static output feedback. IEEE Trans. on Automat. Control, vol. 57, No. 6, p. 1377-1390, 2012.</reference>
                <reference>Gahinet P., Apkarian P. A linear matrix inequality approach to H∞-control. International Journal of Robust and Nonlinear Control, vol. 4, p. 421-448, 1994.</reference>
                <reference>El Ghaoui L., Oustry F., Aitrami M. A cone complementarity linearization algorithm for static output-feedback and related problems. IEEE Trans. Automat. Control, vol. 42, No. 8, p. 1171-1176, 1997.</reference>
                <reference>Balandin D.V., Kogan M.M. Synthesis of controllers on the basis of a solution of linear matrix inequalities and a search algorithm fir reciprocal matrices. Automation and Remote Control, vol. 66, p. 74-91, 2005.</reference>
                <reference>Sadabadi M.S., Peaucelle D. From static output feedback to structured robust static output feedback: A survey. Annual Reviews in Control, vol. 42, p. 11-26, 2016.</reference>
                <reference>Shumafov M.M. Stabilization of linear control systems. Pole assignment problem. A survey. Vestnik of Saint Petersburg University. Mathematics. Mechanics. Astronomy, vol. 6 (64), issue 4, p. 564–591, 2019.</reference>
                <reference>Kimura H. On pole placement by gain output feedback. IEEE Trans. Automat. Control, vol. 20, p. 509-519, 1975.</reference>
            </references>
