    <article>
        <articleInfo lang="EN" FirstPage="126" LastPage="138">
            <pdf>26306-jdecp-pchelintsev.pdf</pdf>
            <abstract>The paper considers numerical modeling of a stationary electric field in a rectangular electroplating bath. The original boundary-value problem for the Laplace equation includes insulating boundary conditions on the bath walls, a prescribed potential on the anode, and a nonlinear cathode condition relating the normal derivative of the potential to the cathodic polarization curve. The method of lines is used: finite-difference approximation is introduced only along the coordinate directed along the electrodes, and the two-dimensional problem is reduced to a boundary-value problem for a system of ordinary differential equations. The system is solved by a numerical-analytical power-series method, while unknown initial data are found by a shooting procedure based on minimization of the boundary residual function. Special attention is paid to the numerical experiment for a bath with a flat anode and cathode. The paper presents computational parameters, a stopping criterion for the power series, the calculated distribution of nickel coating thickness, and an estimate explaining why arbitrary-precision arithmetic is required when the size of the computational domain increases.</abstract>
            <title>High-precision modeling of an electric field in an electroplating bath by the method of lines and power series</title>
            <keywords>
                <word>Laplace equation</word>
                <word>electroplating bath</word>
                <word>method of lines</word>
                <word>power series</word>
                <word>shooting method</word>
                <word>arbitrary-precision arithmetic</word>
                <word>numerical experiment</word>
            </keywords>
            <references>
